{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fastctx","tool":"read","arguments":{"files":[{"path":"C:/jimuyun/execution-plans/2026-08-07-bootstrap-review-operability-hardening/bootstrap-runs/r2/artifact-view/tree/.agents/skills/run-phase-bootstrap-review/SKILL.md","offset":1,"limit":220},{"path":"C:/jimuyun/execution-plans/2026-08-07-bootstrap-review-operability-hardening/bootstrap-runs/r2/artifact-view/tree/docs/adr/ADR-0041-bootstrap-review-execution-control-plane-ownership.md"},{"path":"C:/jimuyun/execution-plans/2026-08-07-bootstrap-review-operability-hardening/bootstrap-runs/r2/artifact-view/tree/docs/adr/ADR-0051-bootstrap-lineage-family-and-bounded-repair-reentry.md"},{"path":"C:/jimuyun/execution-plans/2026-08-07-bootstrap-review-operability-hardening/bootstrap-runs/r2/artifact-view/tree/docs/adr/ADR-0056-ai-native-single-maintainer-finding-mode.md"}]},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"=== C:/jimuyun/execution-plans/2026-08-07-bootstrap-review-operability-hardening/bootstrap-runs/r2/artifact-view/tree/.agents/skills/run-phase-bootstrap-review/SKILL.md (lines 1-220 of 428) ===\n1\t---\n2\tname: run-phase-bootstrap-review\n3\tdescription: Prepare, execute, gate, resume, inspect, seal, or finalize Ji Mu Yun evidence-gated Bootstrap Reviews for plan authority, implementation conformance, Skills/routes, and focused changes. Use for Bootstrap Review, Whole-directory review, Blind Hunter / Edge Case Hunter / Acceptance Auditor orchestration, independent blocker verification, repair-round closure, or recovery of an existing review run.\n4\t---\n5\t\n6\t# Run Phase Bootstrap Review\n7\t\n8\tUse the repository-owned control plane. This Skill owns the executable protocol and generic schemas; it does not own the reviewed plan's business acceptance, commit, handoff, release, or done state.\n9\t\n10\t## Authority Order\n11\t\n12\tRead these before operating the workflow:\n13\t\n14\t1. `docs/adr/ADR-0041-bootstrap-review-execution-control-plane-ownership.md`.\n15\t2. `docs/adr/ADR-0051-bootstrap-lineage-family-and-bounded-repair-reentry.md`.\n16\t3. `docs/adr/ADR-0056-ai-native-single-maintainer-finding-mode.md`.\n17\t4. `docs/standards/bootstrap-review-control-plane.md`.\n18\t5. `references/review-profiles.v1.json`.\n19\t6. `execution-plans/2026-07-12-llm-review-evidence-gate-hardening/09-bootstrap-review-operator-guide.md` for migration examples.\n20\t\n21\tThe repository entrypoint is:\n22\t\n23\t```text\n24\tpy -3 .agents/skills/run-phase-bootstrap-review/scripts/bootstrap_review.py\n25\t```\n26\t\n27\tThe 2026-07-12 CLI is a revision-bound compatibility adapter only. Do not add durable behavior there.\n28\t\n29\t## Hard Boundaries\n30\t\n31\t- Keep Blind Hunter, Edge Case Hunter, and Acceptance Auditor isolated. Do not share candidates or suspected findings before gate.\n32\t- Require complete artifact and context coverage. Sampling is prohibited and zero findings are valid.\n33\t- Use an independent verifier for every accepted P0/P1.\n34\t- Use exactly one `focused_repair_verifier` for a new non-escalated Round 1 repair. It is distinct from all discovery roles and from `independent_verifier`.\n35\t- Treat `ai-native-single-maintainer` as first-class review context. Candidates based on multi-maintainer concurrency or external requirement injection are parent-adjusted P0 to P1, P1 to non-blocking P2, and P2 to ignored; runtime product risks are unchanged.\n36\t- Allow finding discovery automatically only for Round 1. A later complete discovery requires a typed Acceptance trigger, the orchestrator's explicit recommendation and confidence, explicit user confirmation, and a CLI-produced hash-bound re-entry authorization. Prompt text alone never authorizes finding mode.\n37\t- Treat the recommendation as advisory, not a veto. After the user sees the trigger, expected benefit/cost, recommendation, and confidence, explicit confirmation may authorize re-entry even when the recommendation is `do_not_recommend`.\n38\t- A `focused_repair_verifier` verifies only predecessor findings. It must return no new blockers and cannot reopen discovery.\n39\t- Use one stable `lineageFamilyId` for the same acceptance target. Its default budget is two semantic rounds and its hard limit is three; changing `reviewId`, `changeId`, or successor directory never resets it.\n40\t- Do not mutate reviewed files while a review run is active.\n41\t- Never edit reviewer, verifier, gate, process-event, or final evidence to make a run pass.\n42\t- Do not treat Bootstrap output as protected handoff, production gateway, commit, release, or plan-local validator authority.\n43\t- The runner has no hidden provider dispatch. A fallback model is a new explicit operator command after the prior attempt is recorded.\n44\t- Subprocesses use argument arrays, `shell=False`, the environment allowlist, and typed placeholders from the control-plane revision.\n45\t- Codex Exec uses the current attempt directory as its isolated `workspace-write` root for handshake and candidate evidence. The repository root and formal output paths must remain outside the child workspace.\n46\t- Process events are execution facts. `process-leases.json` is a compatibility view derived from those facts.\n47\t- Reviewer launch uses a lock-serialized `attempt-reserved` event before `Popen`; overlapping formal write sets and already-completed roles fail before a child starts, while non-overlapping roles remain concurrent.\n48\t- The runner refreshes that view immediately after `attempt-started`. A dead event-backed PID must append `attempt-stale` before rebuilding the view; changing only the lease sidecar is insufficient.\n49\t- The process-event lock is owner-bound by PID, process creation identity, token, timestamp, and acquired/committed phase. Recovery removes a dead/reused, incomplete stale, or committed owner lock; cleanup retries transient Windows sharing violations and may unlink only its own token.\n50\t\n51\t## Select One Profile\n52\t\n53\t- Plan or Whole-directory authority: `bootstrap-upstream-plan`.\n54\t- Implemented code and feature closure: `bootstrap-implementation-conformance`.\n55\t- Skill or route: `bootstrap-skill-route`.\n56\t- Focused bugfix or bounded change: `bootstrap-focused-change`.\n57\t\n58\tBind every required context class to real in-scope artifacts. For implementation conformance, bind every plan-mandated deterministic check with `--required-check`.\n59\t\n60\t## Inspect Before Starting\n61\t\n62\tWhen a run may already exist, use read-only recovery first:\n63\t\n64\t```text\n65\tpy -3 .agents/skills/run-phase-bootstrap-review/scripts/bootstrap_review.py list-runs --repository-root <repo>\n66\tpy -3 .agents/skills/run-phase-bootstrap-review/scripts/bootstrap_review.py inspect-lineage --repository-root <repo> --lineage-family-id <family>\n67\tpy -3 .agents/skills/run-phase-bootstrap-review/scripts/bootstrap_review.py inspect-run --run-dir <run>\n68\t```\n69\t\n70\tFor cost/yield governance, build a non-authorizing baseline from the validated\n71\tregistry or an explicit set of formal run directories:\n72\t\n73\t```text\n74\tpy -3 .agents/skills/run-phase-bootstrap-review/scripts/bootstrap_review.py build-review-baseline \\\n75\t  --repository-root <repo> --out-dir logs/review-governance/baselines/<id> \\\n76\t  [--run-dir <formal-run> ...]\n77\t```\n78\t\n79\tThe history index includes every located lifecycle state. Only finalized runs\n80\tthat pass full evidence replay enter semantic statistics, and only those with\n81\tcomplete structured attempt token and process-event evidence enter cost\n82\tcohorts. Token counts come from the same Codex JSONL execution, every counted\n83\tattempt directory must match a started and terminal process-event lifecycle,\n84\tand each selected model must match the role-specific route frozen by the run.\n85\tA single-model route keeps the model name as its cohort identity. A legitimate\n86\tmixed route, such as Terra discovery plus Sol verification, uses a canonical\n87\thash of the complete configured role route and is never attributed to either\n88\tsingle-model cohort. The history index and calibration candidate publish as\n89\tone staged directory. A finalized run that fails replay fails the complete\n90\tbuild. Generated\n91\tcalibration is a candidate and never updates the promoted reference\n92\tautomatically. Baselines and calibration always carry `authorizes=[]`.\n93\t\n94\tPass the hash-bound `inspect-lineage` result to the consumer even when it\n95\treports zero rounds; omission must never mean a fresh family. Follow the\n96\treported `nextAction`. Do not infer current state from an old assistant summary.\n97\tThe local Git registry is only a performance index: every registered manifest\n98\tand adoption record is re-hashed before use, and damaged registered history\n99\tfails closed. The index binds its source Git revision. A changed HEAD triggers\n100\tone repository reconciliation scan; controller-created runs and adoptions at\n101\tthe same HEAD are registered explicitly, so ordinary inspection does not scan\n102\tthe repository repeatedly.\n103\tWhen canonical profile content changes, add the outgoing revision and its\n104\tauthority-root binding to `references/historical-policy-revisions.v1.json` in\n105\tthe same change. The archive exists only to replay historical typed decisions;\n106\tit cannot prepare a new run. Historical replay reconstructs the content-addressed\n107\tprofile from the frozen manifest plus registered extensions and rejects any\n108\thash mismatch. Use `baseline-only` for revisions that lack successor authority;\n109\tonly `baseline-and-successor` entries with a registered authority root may\n110\tauthorize successor-policy resolution.\n111\t\n112\tWhen pre-family history must count toward a target-derived family, use\n113\t`adopt-lineage` once with explicit historical run directories and a hash-bound\n114\tAccepted policy authority. Never rewrite old `review-input.json` files or infer\n115\tadoption from directory proximity. One historical run cannot be adopted by\n116\tmultiple families.\n117\t\n118\t## Prepare\n119\t\n120\t`prepare --dry-run` is a non-authorizing binding preview. Its\n121\t`closureBindings` object mirrors the individual candidate, source, validator,\n122\tGit, write-set, execution-read-set, and dependency-closure hashes in the\n123\tdiagnostic output. The preview must report `authorizes: []` and must not create\n124\ta run directory; mismatched scope or context inputs remain field-level\n125\tdiagnostics and fail closed before any model launch. Context diagnostics also\n126\texpose `acceptedScopesByClass` so each required class can be traced to the\n127\tprepared artifacts that satisfy it.\n128\t\n129\tCreate a new run with stable `reviewId`, `changeId`, `lineageFamilyId`, round, profile, scopes, context classes, execution mode, and exclusivity attestation. Derive the family from the original acceptance target and retain it for every in-place repair or successor that still evaluates that target. New runs require `--lineage-family-id`.\n130\t\n131\tDeclare:\n132\t\n133\t- `--write-set` for paths the reviewed change may write.\n134\t- `--execution-read-set` for validator/runtime inputs already present in scope.\n135\t- `--dependency` for the execution dependency closure already present in scope.\n136\t\n137\tUse the smallest complete consumer closure. `bootstrap-upstream-plan` may bind\n138\tthe whole plan directory because that directory is the review object. For\n139\timplementation, Skill-route, and focused-change profiles, prefer explicit\n140\tchanged files, direct contracts and consumers, targeted tests, repository\n141\trules, referenced standards, and current acceptance evidence. If a directory\n142\tis genuinely the minimal complete closure, prepare requires\n143\t`--directory-scope-attestation directory-is-minimal-complete-closure`.\n144\t\n145\tRound 2 and Round 3 also require `--predecessor-run-dir` and a hash-bound `--repair-closure`. The closure must cover the exact finalized predecessor finding set, including confirmed, advisory, and refuted dispositions. A later complete discovery also requires `--round-entry-reason` with `novel_p0_p1`, `authority_context_graph_changed`, or `high_risk_boundary_changed`; the last reason also requires explicit changed `--high-risk-boundary` artifacts.\n146\t\n147\tFor `bootstrap-implementation-conformance`, repair rounds also require\n148\t`--acceptance-repair-route <route.json>` and\n149\t`--acceptance-repair-completeness <projection.json>`. Use the exact output of\n150\tAcceptance `prepare-bootstrap` and its replayed completeness projection. The\n151\tcontroller binds both files, family, consumed round, next round, and typed\n152\tentry reason before preparing reviewer material.\n153\t\n154\tWhen that route selects complete discovery after Round 1, show the user the\n155\ttyped trigger, expected benefit/cost, your `recommend` or `do_not_recommend`\n156\tjudgement, and a confidence in `[0,1]`. Only after explicit confirmation run:\n157\t\n158\t```text\n159\tpy -3 .agents/skills/run-phase-bootstrap-review/scripts/bootstrap_review.py authorize-finding-mode-reentry \\\n160\t  --repository-root <repo> --lineage-family-id <family> --next-review-round <round> \\\n161\t  --predecessor-run-dir <run> [--acceptance-repair-route <route.json>] \\\n162\t  --round-entry-reason <typed-trigger> --recommendation <recommend|do_not_recommend> \\\n163\t  --confidence <0..1> --rationale <reason> --user-confirmed --out <authorization.json>\n164\t```\n165\t\n166\tImplementation-conformance re-entry requires the Acceptance route argument;\n167\tstandalone plan/Skill review instead relies on Bootstrap's deterministic typed\n168\tentry validation. Pass the append-only result to `prepare --finding-mode-reentry-authorization\n169\t<authorization.json>`. The CLI revalidates its lineage, predecessor, round,\n170\ttyped trigger, Acceptance route hash, recommendation, confidence, and user\n171\tconfirmation. Without it, later discovery fails before prompts or processes.\n172\t\n173\tFor Round 1 implementation conformance, pass the Acceptance-owned prepared\n174\tinput as `--acceptance-run-input`. A nonexistent `--scope` is valid only when\n175\tthe replayed candidate identity declares that exact path deleted, the path is\n176\tstill absent, and its hash matches bytes read from the custody-bound immutable\n177\tGit baseline. The public manifest never accepts a caller snapshot or source\n178\tpath. For repair rounds, a truly deleted predecessor artifact continues to be\n179\tinferred from frozen predecessor evidence and current absence; do not pass that\n180\trepair-only deletion as a nonexistent `--scope`. Both forms enter the Artifact\n181\tView deleted tree and remain formal reviewer evidence.\n182\t\n183\tPrepare freezes Git HEAD, Git index, direct artifacts, execution read set, dependency closure, context graph, cost estimate, and, for Codex Exec, `artifact-view.v1`. A replacement for a stale run uses a new review ID and fresh snapshot; the new run does not inherit stale state.\n184\t\n185\tWhen knowledge context is supplied, prepare evaluates adapter-owned consumption decisions before freezing the Artifact View. Only accepted decisions with nonempty satisfied context classes may augment required context; rejected decisions remain metadata and cannot satisfy profile completeness. Children never invoke Locator after prepare.\n186\t\n187\tPass the VDD-owned file through `prepare --knowledge-context <repo-relative-path>`.\n188\tPrepare verifies every accepted candidate is already in scope with the exact\n189\tsource hash, then records the context hash and selected candidates in the\n190\tfrozen manifest. Bootstrap does not create a new Locator query or promote a\n191\tcandidate on its own.\n192\t\n193\tPrepare also requires the adjacent VDD freeze receipt, verifies both files from\n194\tthe same bytes collected into scope, and binds every path in each accepted\n195\tcandidate's complete Locator read-set into the Artifact View and mapped context\n196\tclasses. The shared validator must byte-match current main.\n197\t\n198\tThe shared context validator also verifies the current publication pointer,\n199\timmutable generation manifest, Catalog/policy/projection bindings, and current\n200\tmain source hashes. A missing, stale, or tampered publication blocks prepare.\n201\tNo Bootstrap child may use the staging-only `--allow-unpublished-inputs` path.\n202\t\n203\tAn abandoned Codex Exec run with no gate and incomplete required layers may be replaced at the same round after repairing the execution defect. Preserve the abandoned evidence and carry any valid partial finding into repair evidence or the replacement review scope; this exception does not reset a gated or completed semantic round.\n204\t\n205\t## Complete Deterministic Preflight\n206\t\n207\tRun every check from `review-input.json.deterministicPreflightPolicy`. Save command, exit code, output path, and output hash under `<run>/preflight/`, then complete `preflight-result.json`.\n208\t\n209\tStop before semantic reviewers when a required check fails or evidence is stale.\n210\t\n211\t## Prove Codex Access\n212\t\n213\tFor `codex-exec`, run the identity-equivalent access probe after preflight and before authorization. Use `--role discovery` for complete discovery and `--role focused_repair_verifier` for focused verification:\n214\t\n215\t```text\n216\tpy -3 .agents/skills/run-phase-bootstrap-review/scripts/bootstrap_review.py prove-access \\\n217\t  --run-dir <run> \\\n218\t  --codex-command <codex executable> \\\n219\t  --role <discovery|focused_repair_verifier> \\\n220\t  --model <profile-allowed model> \\\n\n=== C:/jimuyun/execution-plans/2026-08-07-bootstrap-review-operability-hardening/bootstrap-runs/r2/artifact-view/tree/docs/adr/ADR-0041-bootstrap-review-execution-control-plane-ownership.md (lines 1-44 of 44) ===\n1\t# ADR-0041: Bootstrap Review Execution Control Plane Ownership\n2\t\n3\t- Status: Accepted\n4\t- Date: 2026-07-16\n5\t\n6\t## Context\n7\t\n8\tBootstrap Review started as a plan-local manual review gateway. Repeated real runs proved the semantic review model useful, but process launch, artifact access, lease handling, retries, recovery, and lifecycle inspection became duplicated across temporary run helpers and a historical execution-plan directory.\n9\t\n10\tA top-level router would add stateful dispatch without resolving authority. Extending historical script directories would also blur durable protocol ownership, plan-specific acceptance, and append-only evidence.\n11\t\n12\t## Decision\n13\t\n14\tBootstrap Review uses a documented protocol with stateless adapters and explicit ownership boundaries.\n15\t\n16\t- `docs/standards/bootstrap-review-control-plane.md` owns durable semantics, authority, lifecycle, and recovery rules.\n17\t- `.agents/skills/run-phase-bootstrap-review/` owns executable protocol, generic schemas, Artifact View, the Codex Exec runner, attempt/event records, repair closure, lifecycle inspection, and common validation tools.\n18\t- `execution-plans/<plan>/` owns contract instances, implementation-contract instances, plan-specific predicates, fixtures, and current-plan evidence. A plan-local validator remains the business acceptance authority for that plan.\n19\t- `execution-plans/2026-07-12-llm-review-evidence-gate-hardening/` is a compatibility adapter and migration fixture, not the durable implementation owner.\n20\t- `logs/` owns append-only run, attempt, review, audit, and recovery evidence.\n21\t- The external global `run-phase-bootstrap-review` Skill is a revision-bound thin route into the repository-owned Skill.\n22\t- Profile-declared formal companions, including `acceptance-inventory-attestation@1.0`, remain repository-owned Bootstrap protocol capabilities. Consumers bind and import their outputs but cannot create a competing reviewer, schema, launch authorization, or Artifact View.\n23\t\n24\tThe runner is an execution backend only. It does not own candidate acceptance, severity, review completion, done state, commit, handoff, or release authority. It must not become a hidden provider scheduler and must support evidence-based recovery without hidden mutable state.\n25\t\n26\tSubprocess invocation uses argument arrays with shell execution disabled, an explicit environment-variable allowlist, and typed placeholders. Concurrency blocks only overlapping write sets, while freezing the Git index and detecting concurrent authority drift across the execution read set and dependency closure.\n27\t\n28\tNew plans formally bootstrap the protocol. Historical plans may receive read-only backfill and shadow validation only. A new run may supersede a stale run, but it starts from a fresh authority snapshot and is not itself born stale.\n29\t\n30\t## Consequences\n31\t\n32\t- Protocol changes are made once in the repository-owned Skill and projected through compatibility adapters.\n33\t- Plan-local validators retain domain acceptance authority.\n34\t- Generic schemas cannot drift independently across plans.\n35\t- Historical evidence remains immutable; annotations and indexes are additive and reproducible.\n36\t- Reviews cannot close with an open accepted P0/P1. Every P2 must have an explicit disposition; high-risk P2 cannot be deferred, and expired deferrals block automatically.\n37\t- This ADR is superseded only when these ownership or authority decisions change. Ordinary execution plans reference it instead of creating another ownership ADR.\n38\t\n39\t## References\n40\t\n41\t- `decision-logs/2026-07-16-bootstrap-review-self-audit-and-hardening-proposal.md`\n42\t- `docs/standards/bootstrap-review-control-plane.md`\n43\t- `execution-plans/2026-07-12-llm-review-evidence-gate-hardening/09-bootstrap-review-operator-guide.md`\n44\t\n\n=== C:/jimuyun/execution-plans/2026-08-07-bootstrap-review-operability-hardening/bootstrap-runs/r2/artifact-view/tree/docs/adr/ADR-0051-bootstrap-lineage-family-and-bounded-repair-reentry.md (lines 1-105 of 105) ===\n1\t# ADR-0051: Bootstrap Lineage Family And Bounded Repair Re-entry\n2\t\n3\t- Status: Accepted\n4\t- Date: 2026-07-30\n5\t\n6\t## Context\n7\t\n8\tADR-0041 established the repository-owned Bootstrap control plane, ADR-0045\n9\tmade verifier publication recoverable, and ADR-0049 separated transport\n10\tattempts from semantic rounds. Repeated implementation-acceptance work still\n11\tused a new `changeId` or successor policy after each repair batch. That made\n12\tone unchanged acceptance target appear to have a fresh three-round budget and\n13\tcaused full high-cost reviews to repeat without a corresponding change in\n14\tauthority, risk, or consumer scope.\n15\t\n16\tThe repository is maintained by one person with AI assistance. The needed\n17\tcontrol is deterministic lineage and bounded re-entry, not reviewer signatures\n18\tor multi-writer governance.\n19\t\n20\t## Decision\n21\t\n22\tBootstrap semantic-round accounting uses a stable lineage family.\n23\t\n24\t- Every new review declares `lineageFamilyId`. A consumer derives it from the\n25\t  original acceptance target and retains it across in-place repair directories,\n26\t  renamed `changeId` values, and successor history that still evaluates that\n27\t  target.\n28\t- Historical manifests without the field remain read-only compatible by using\n29\t  their existing `changeId` as the effective family. New runs fail closed when\n30\t  the family is absent.\n31\t- Legacy `changeId` history enters a target-derived family only through an\n32\t  append-only lineage-adoption record. The record binds explicitly selected\n33\t  historical run manifests and an Accepted policy authority by content hash;\n34\t  historical manifests remain unchanged and cannot be adopted by competing\n35\t  families.\n36\t- Bootstrap keeps a Git-local run registry only as a performance index. Every\n37\t  registered run and adoption is re-hashed and reparsed before use. Missing,\n38\t  stale, or malformed registered history fails closed; the registry does not\n39\t  become semantic authority.\n40\t- A family has a default budget of two semantic rounds and a hard limit of\n41\t  three. A round is consumed only when semantic reviewer execution starts;\n42\t  failed access probes and transport attempts stay in the same round.\n43\t- Round 1 reviews the minimal complete closure. Round 2 reviews the bounded\n44\t  repair delta and its reachable consumers while preserving required authority\n45\t  context. It does not rediscover unrelated repository areas.\n46\t- Round 3 requires a typed entry decision: `novel_p0_p1`,\n47\t  `authority_context_graph_changed`, or `high_risk_boundary_changed`. A clean\n48\t  or P2-only predecessor never opens another semantic round.\n49\t- A successor does not reset the family budget. After the hard limit the family\n50\t  enters `manual_pause`. Only an explicit supersede or incompatible-scope\n51\t  decision that creates a genuinely different acceptance target may derive a\n52\t  new family; a successor policy alone cannot make the old target new.\n53\t- `inspect-lineage` is the hash-bound reproducible view of consumed rounds and\n54\t  the next available round. Consumers require it even when the count is zero;\n55\t  omission cannot mean a fresh budget. Derived Artifact View, acceptance\n56\t  snapshot, and nested worktree copies are not review-history authority.\n57\t  `changeId` remains a change label, not a budget identity.\n58\t\n59\tImplementation-acceptance repair re-entry requires deterministic completeness\n60\tevidence before another review route is selected.\n61\t\n62\t- Quick Dev emits a non-authorizing handoff with changed files, direct\n63\t  consumers, targeted tests, validation references, generated root-cause\n64\t  callsite inventories, and controlled producer/consumer composition receipts.\n65\t- Acceptance binds present changed files by content hash and deleted files by\n66\t  explicit tombstone before choosing a review route. Every composition check\n67\t  covers a changed path, uses a declared direct consumer, and binds its receipt\n68\t  as a validation reference. All repair evidence paths must belong to the same\n69\t  hash-bound minimal review closure, with consumers and tests in their declared\n70\t  context classes.\n71\t- The Acceptance Skill audits that every discovered sibling callsite is changed\n72\t  or explicitly excluded and that each composition command succeeded. It then\n73\t  selects `deterministic_only`, `focused_repair_review`,\n74\t  `full_implementation_conformance`, or `manual_pause`.\n75\t- After two consumed rounds, a complete repair with no typed Round 3 trigger\n76\t  uses deterministic closure. The plan-local acceptance predicate remains the\n77\t  authority; neither the handoff nor the route authorizes completion.\n78\t\n79\tThis ADR extends ADR-0041, complements ADR-0045 and ADR-0049, and supersedes\n80\tnone of them. It does not change reviewer isolation, independent P0/P1\n81\tverification, provider routing, protected handoff, commit, release, or\n82\tplan-local acceptance authority.\n83\t\n84\t## Consequences\n85\t\n86\t- Renaming a change or creating a successor directory no longer creates an\n87\t  unbounded high-cost review loop for the same acceptance target.\n88\t- Repair review input becomes smaller while retaining direct consumer and\n89\t  authority context needed to find regressions caused by the repair.\n90\t- A third review is exceptional and machine-explainable.\n91\t- Existing review and successor evidence remains immutable and readable.\n92\t- Consumers must carry one stable family and explicit repair-completeness\n93\t  evidence; omitting either fails closed before a new semantic launch.\n94\t\n95\t## References\n96\t\n97\t- `docs/adr/ADR-0041-bootstrap-review-execution-control-plane-ownership.md`\n98\t- `docs/adr/ADR-0045-bootstrap-verifier-semantic-commit-and-recovery.md`\n99\t- `docs/adr/ADR-0049-bootstrap-controller-owned-coverage-and-attempt-retry.md`\n100\t- `docs/standards/bootstrap-review-control-plane.md`\n101\t- `.agents/skills/run-phase-bootstrap-review/SKILL.md`\n102\t- `.agents/skills/run-refactor-implementation-acceptance/SKILL.md`\n103\t- `.agents/skills/vdd-execution-plan/SKILL.md`\n104\t- `.agents/skills/quick-dev-tdd-adapter/SKILL.md`\n105\t\n\n=== C:/jimuyun/execution-plans/2026-08-07-bootstrap-review-operability-hardening/bootstrap-runs/r2/artifact-view/tree/docs/adr/ADR-0056-ai-native-single-maintainer-finding-mode.md (lines 1-77 of 77) ===\n1\t# ADR-0056: AI-Native Single-Maintainer Finding Mode\n2\t\n3\t- Status: Accepted\n4\t- Date: 2026-08-02\n5\t- Amended: 2026-08-06\n6\t\n7\t## Context\n8\t\n9\tBootstrap Review and Refactor Acceptance serve an AI-led repository maintained\n10\tby one person. Reviewers were treating multi-maintainer races and external\n11\trequirement injection as ordinary product threats, and focused repair review\n12\tcould reopen discovery without a distinct user decision. Prompt guidance alone\n13\tdid not provide a deterministic enforcement boundary.\n14\t\n15\t## Decision\n16\t\n17\t- New Bootstrap and Acceptance routes freeze\n18\t  `maintenanceMode=ai-native-single-maintainer` and the same severity policy.\n19\t- A candidate classified as multi-maintainer concurrency or external\n20\t  requirement injection shifts P0 to P1, P1 to non-blocking P2, and P2 to\n21\t  ignored. Runtime product risk is unchanged. Bootstrap's parent CLI applies\n22\t  the shift before gate aggregation.\n23\t- Round 1 discovery is automatically eligible. Later complete discovery remains\n24\t  available only for a typed `novel_p0_p1`,\n25\t  `authority_context_graph_changed`, or `high_risk_boundary_changed` route.\n26\t- Before later discovery, the orchestrator reports its recommendation,\n27\t  confidence, expected benefit, and cost. Explicit user confirmation is recorded\n28\t  through an append-only Bootstrap CLI authorization bound to the lineage,\n29\t  predecessor, round, trigger, and Acceptance route hash. The recommendation is\n30\t  advisory: a user may explicitly confirm re-entry even when the orchestrator\n31\t  records `do_not_recommend`.\n32\t- The maintainer may instead explicitly decline a Round 2 discovery proposal.\n33\t  Refactor Acceptance may then close deterministically only when the current\n34\t  repair-completeness producer replays successfully, no novel P0/P1 exists,\n35\t  exactly one round was consumed, and the proposal was caused only by a\n36\t  repair-derived authority/context or high-risk-boundary change. The closure\n37\t  records residual-risk acceptance, publishes `acceptance-passed`, and does\n38\t  not authorize commit, release, or archive.\n39\t- `prepare`, access proof, and layer launch fail closed when finding mode or its\n40\t  re-entry authorization is absent, stale, or substituted.\n41\t- Focused repair verification covers only predecessor findings. It cannot emit\n42\t  new findings or escalation triggers; a still-broken predecessor finding\n43\t  remains blocking.\n44\t- Round 1-2 discovery, including Skill/route review, uses Terra at the\n45\t  profile-declared effort. Every valid Round 3 remains Sol/high.\n46\t- New candidates classify verifier risk independently from severity as\n47\t  `standard`, `authority_control`, `lifecycle_control`, `protected_path`, or\n48\t  `shared_entrypoint`. Standard P1 verification uses Terra/high; a high-risk P1\n49\t  uses Sol/high; P0 or `security` uses Sol/max. Focused repair derives the same\n50\t  route from its frozen predecessor finding set.\n51\t- Historical run bytes remain read-only compatible under their frozen policy.\n52\t\n53\tThis ADR refines ADR-0051 and ADR-0055 and supersedes their statements that a\n54\tfocused verifier may originate an escalation trigger. It does not change the\n55\tthree-round hard limit, independent verification of accepted P0/P1 findings,\n56\tor the explicit no-hidden-provider-dispatch boundary.\n57\t\n58\t## Consequences\n59\t\n60\t- Repository-specific threat assumptions affect severity deterministically,\n61\t  not through reviewer discretion alone.\n62\t- A second discovery pass is possible but visible, justified, and explicitly\n63\t  user-approved.\n64\t- A declined second discovery pass has a narrow, hash-bound deterministic exit\n65\t  instead of becoming an acceptance dead end.\n66\t- Ordinary precise repair stays on the single focused verifier path.\n67\t- Routine review and verification cost falls to Terra while Sol remains bound\n68\t  to late-round or structured high-risk evidence.\n69\t\n70\t## References\n71\t\n72\t- `docs/adr/ADR-0051-bootstrap-lineage-family-and-bounded-repair-reentry.md`\n73\t- `docs/adr/ADR-0055-focused-repair-verification-and-lightweight-closure.md`\n74\t- `docs/standards/bootstrap-review-control-plane.md`\n75\t- `.agents/skills/run-phase-bootstrap-review/SKILL.md`\n76\t- `.agents/skills/run-refactor-implementation-acceptance/SKILL.md`\n77\t\n\n(Partial: 3 of 4 files processed. Continue with files=[{\"path\":\"C:/jimuyun/execution-plans/2026-08-07-bootstrap-review-operability-hardening/bootstrap-runs/r2/artifact-view/tree/.agents/skills/run-phase-bootstrap-review/SKILL.md\",\"offset\":221}].)"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
